## Testing Requirements

### Before Committing:
1. Build succeeds (use the dotnet command above)
2. No compiler errors
3. No compiler warnings (except VS version warning)
4. Test in-editor if gameplay code changed
5. Verify network replication if multiplayer code changed

### Test Scenarios:
- Enemy AI detects and engages player
- Station input routing (WASD → mech)
- Damage system spawns hazards
- Procedural generation creates geometry

---

## Performance Considerations

### Tick Optimization:
- Use timers for periodic checks (not Tick)
- AI scans at 1 Hz, not 60 Hz
- Disable tick on components that don't need it
- Use `PrimaryActorTick.bCanEverTick = false` when possible

### Example:
```cpp
// BAD - Every frame
void Tick(float DeltaTime) {
    FindNearestTarget();  // Expensive!
}

// GOOD - Once per second
void BeginPlay() {
    GetWorld()->GetTimerManager().SetTimer(
        ScanTimerHandle,
        this,
        &AEnemyMechPawn::FindNearestTarget,
        1.0f,  // 1 second interval
        true   // Looping
    );
}
```

---

## Common Pitfalls

### 1. Don't call SetupAttachment on UActorComponent
```cpp
// WRONG
UMechMovementComponent* Movement = CreateDefaultSubobject<...>();
Movement->SetupAttachment(RootComponent);  // ERROR!

// RIGHT
UMechMovementComponent* Movement = CreateDefaultSubobject<...>();
// No attachment needed for non-scene components
```

### 2. Don't access components in Constructor
```cpp
// WRONG - Components not fully initialized
APlayerMechPawn::APlayerMechPawn() {
    MechMovement->SetSpeed(100.0f);  // Crash!
}

// RIGHT - Use BeginPlay
void APlayerMechPawn::BeginPlay() {
    Super::BeginPlay();
    MechMovement->SetSpeed(100.0f);  // Safe
}
```

### 3. Always check pointers before use
```cpp
// WRONG
void Fire() {
    WeaponSystem->FireWeapon();  // Crash if nullptr!
}

// RIGHT
void Fire() {
    if (WeaponSystem) {
        WeaponSystem->FireWeapon();
    }
}
```

